# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Example Kitchen — production HOST env templates (plain docker compose)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   The irreducible host-side bootstrap each stack needs to come up under plain
#   `docker compose`. Every actual SECRET (SESSION_SECRET, CLOVER_*, the *_ADMIN/
#   *_INTERNAL tokens, CREW_*, APP_SETTING_ENCRYPTION_KEY, AI_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY,
#   DEEPSEEK_API_KEY, …) is injected at container boot by the `with-infisical`
#   entrypoint from Infisical (ADR-0020 + machine identity, PR #521). DO NOT put
#   those here — leave them to Infisical.
#
# HOW IT'S USED
#   Each stack is brought up on the host with its OWN env file:
#     docker compose -f compose/kds.yml --env-file compose/kds.env up -d
#   Copy each section below into a SEPARATE, gitignored file per stack
#   (compose/kds.env, analytics.env, …). One shared file does NOT work:
#   DATABASE_URL / SESSION_SECRET mean different things in the kds vs backoffice
#   stacks. The real files are NEVER committed (see .gitignore).
#
# ⚠️  INFISICAL OVERRIDE RULE — READ THIS
#   `with-infisical` OVERRIDES same-named process env at boot. So any var you set
#   below that ALSO exists in the Infisical `prod` scope gets replaced by the
#   Infisical value. The infra URLs below (DATABASE_URL / REDIS_URL /
#   *_DATABASE_URL) now point at the new LOCAL compose DNS (postgres-kds:5432,
#   redis-kds:6379, …). If a stale overlay-DNS value (e.g. postgres-kds-<suffix>)
#   is left in Infisical it WINS and the container cannot reach its database.
#   → Phase B: REMOVE the infra URLs from Infisical prod (keep them here), OR
#     repoint them in Infisical to the local DNS. Recommended: keep them HERE.
#
# 🔑  SECRET-ZERO
#   INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_ID / _SECRET authenticate the stack to Infisical
#   (Universal Auth machine identity "Claude"). They are the ONLY real credential
#   on the host. Get them from Infisical → Access Control → Machine Identities.
#   (The legacy static INFISICAL_TOKEN was revoked — leave it unset.)

# ═══════════════════════════════ kds.env ════════════════════════════════
# Owns postgres-kds + redis-kds (also consumed by backoffice and crm-worker).
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_ID=
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Local datastore credential (fresh volume — pick anything; not network-exposed).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
# Infra URLs → local compose DNS. Keep OUT of Infisical (see override rule).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres-kds:5432/rop_kds
REDIS_URL=redis://redis-kds:6379
# Non-secret config consumed directly by kds-api. Set here UNLESS already served
# from your Infisical prod scope (harmless to set in both for non-secrets).
APP_BASE_URL=https://kds.example.com
WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=example.com
WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=Example Kitchen
BOARD_TIMEZONE=America/Chicago
CLOVER_REGION=us
CLOVER_BASE_URL=https://api.clover.com
# CLOVER_APP_ID / CLOVER_APP_SECRET are secrets → leave to Infisical.

# ═══════════════════════════ analytics.env ══════════════════════════════
# Owns postgres-analytics (its own DB — Iron Law 1/5).
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_ID=
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres-analytics:5432/rop_analytics

# ═══════════════════════════ backoffice.env ═════════════════════════════
# No DB of its own: CONSUMES the kds stack's postgres-kds (shared auth store) +
# redis-kds over rop-net. These two MUST match kds.env exactly.
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_ID=
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_SECRET=
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres-kds:5432/rop_kds
REDIS_URL=redis://redis-kds:6379
WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=example.com
WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=Example Kitchen Backoffice
# Operator bell/inbox → notifications-api. OPT-IN: leave unset to keep the bell on
# its sample seed; set BOTH to go live once the notifications stack is up.
# INTERNAL_NOTIFICATIONS_API_URL=http://notifications-api:4400
# NOTIFICATIONS_INTERNAL_TOKEN → shared secret, leave to Infisical (must match notifications.env).

# ═══════════════════════════ inventory.env ══════════════════════════════
# Owns postgres-inventory (its own DB — Iron Law 1).
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_ID=
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
INVENTORY_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres-inventory:5432/rop_inventory

# ═════════════════════ crm.env (STAGED — not yet live) ══════════════════
# Owns postgres-crm. crm-worker consumes the kds stack's Redis (the cross-app
# loyalty-accrual bus) — CRM_REDIS_URL MUST equal kds.env's REDIS_URL.
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_ID=
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
CRM_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres-crm:5432/rop_crm
CRM_REDIS_URL=redis://redis-kds:6379

# ═════════ notifications.env (STAGED — not yet live) ════════════════════
# Owns postgres-notifications (its own DB — Iron Law 1). Internal-only: no public
# host, no Redis. The backoffice BFF reaches it over rop-net. To actually
# surface real notifications in the bell, ALSO set INTERNAL_NOTIFICATIONS_API_URL
# + NOTIFICATIONS_INTERNAL_TOKEN in backoffice.env (see below).
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_ID=
INFISICAL_MACHINE_CLIENT_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
NOTIFICATIONS_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres-notifications:5432/rop_notifications
# NOTIFICATIONS_INTERNAL_TOKEN is a shared secret → leave to Infisical (must match
# the same-named var in backoffice.env). Min 16 chars.

# ═══════════ crew / order / legal / marketing — no Infisical, no DB ══════
# These four need NO secret-zero and NO datastore. Their only host vars are
# non-secret URLs that already default correctly in the compose files, so they
# can deploy with NO --env-file at all. Override only if the defaults are wrong:
#   crew.env     : INVENTORY_API_BASE_URL=http://inventory-api:4100   (optional)
#   order.env    : ORDER_API_BASE_URL=https://kds.example.com  (optional)
#   legal.env    : (none)
#   marketing.env: (none)
